\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N=\{1,2,\ldots\}$, and $\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$ denote the integers, rationals, reals and complex numbers. Any other conventions are specified locally.


For an affine line $\ell\subset\mathbb R^2$, write $H_\ell^+$ and $H_\ell^-$ for its two open half-planes, in either order. Points on $\ell$ are counted in neither half-plane. For a finite set $X$, its imbalance at $\ell$ is
\[
 b_X(\ell)=\bigl||X\cap H_\ell^+|-|X\cap H_\ell^-|\bigr|.
\]
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
For every integer $n\geq2$ and every $n$-point set $X\subset\mathbb R^2$, does there exist an affine line $\ell$ with $|X\cap\ell|\geq2$ and $b_X(\ell)\leq100$? No general-position assumption is made, and the line may contain more than two points. \href{https://people.maths.ox.ac.uk/greenbj/papers/open-problems.pdf}{[S2]}

\subsection{Short English statement}
Does every finite planar point set admit a line through two of its points with at most one hundred points of imbalance between the two sides?

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{description}
\item[S1] ULAM.AI and the UnsolvedMath Contributors, \emph{UnsolvedMath: A Curated Collection of Open Mathematics Problems}, record 136 (GREEN-048). CC BY 4.0. \url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}.
\item[S2] Ben Green, \emph{100 Open Problems}, maintained author collection, Problem 75 and its comments, accessed 7 October 2026. \url{https://people.maths.ox.ac.uk/greenbj/papers/open-problems.pdf}.
\end{description}
